  "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B003",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B006",
    "B028"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from inflation to optical lattice clocks. The decisive consideration is not a comparison of support, but the unusually complete conjunction of a sharply defined physical principle, exacting experimental realization, independent reproducibility, and a compact attribution. Magic-wavelength confinement made it possible to suppress a central systematic limitation while exploiting many atoms, creating a mature precision-measurement capability with continuing fundamental and metrological reach. A citation confined to optical lattice clocks recognizes Katori's originating concept and Ye's defining experimental and metrological development without claiming credit for the entire optical-clock enterprise.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains essential: the broader optical-clock community contributed to transitions, systematic control, comparisons, and applications, and the committee must establish that no further person is indispensable to the lattice-clock invention and defining development. This is a qualification to investigate, not a reason to expand the citation into a general award for optical timekeeping.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that the magic-wavelength insight, sustained realization, international reproducibility, and the Katori-Ye attribution make optical lattice clocks an exceptionally mature case, while insisting on a boundary from the wider optical-clock field.",
        "response": "I agree with that delimitation. It is precisely the narrowness of the lattice-clock achievement that makes the proposed citation defensible: one can recognize the physical method and its defining development without converting a prize for a discovery into retrospective credit for every subsequent advance in optical frequency standards."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce moved from the experimental Higgs discovery to B003 because lattice clocks offer a distinctive measurement principle, exceptional systematic control, replication, and a more credible individual allocation.",
        "response": "His comparison correctly identifies the issue that matters for an award to named individuals. Great collaborative discoveries remain great physics, but a three-person prize must identify contributions that can be stated honestly at individual scale. B003 meets that test more readily while retaining exceptional scientific substance."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig maintains that KPZ universality has exceptional conceptual breadth and unusually detailed experimental confirmation, but notes the difficulty of separating foundational formulation, later theory, and experimental credit.",
        "response": "The KPZ case remains intellectually powerful, especially because its universal predictions have been tested so precisely. I give B003 priority because its discovery-to-realization chain and two-person core are presently more sharply bounded. That is an attribution judgment, not a denial of the fundamental importance of non-equilibrium universality."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review confirms that Katori and Ye alone are the indispensable recipients for a citation limited to optical lattice clocks.",
      "Whether the citation can remain sufficiently narrow to exclude broader optical-clock advances while still accurately describing Ye's defining metrological development."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
